      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the number of ordered triples of divisors (d\u2081, d\u2082, d\u2083) of 360 such that the product d\u2081d\u2082d\u2083 is also a divisor of 360. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, I remember that 360 has a prime factorization that might be useful here. Let me factorize 360. 360 divided by 2 is 180, divided by 2 again is 90, again by 2 is 45. Then 45 divided by 3 is 15, divided by 3 is 5, and then divided by 5 is 1. So the prime factors are 2\u00b3 * 3\u00b2 * 5\u00b9. So 360 = 2\u00b3 * 3\u00b2 * 5\u00b9.\n\nNow, any divisor of 360 will have exponents for primes 2, 3, 5 that are less than or equal to 3, 2, 1 respectively. So if I consider a divisor d, it can be written as 2^a * 3^b * 5^c where 0 \u2264 a \u2264 3, 0 \u2264 b \u2264 2, 0 \u2264 c \u2264 1.\n\nSo for each of the three divisors d\u2081, d\u2082, d\u2083, they can be represented in terms of exponents of 2, 3, 5. Then, when we take their product d\u2081d\u2082d\u2083, the exponents for each prime will be the sum of the exponents from each divisor. For the product to be a divisor of 360, the exponents of each prime in the product must not exceed those in 360. So for prime 2, the sum of exponents from d\u2081, d\u2082, d\u2083 must be \u2264 3; for prime 3, the sum must be \u2264 2; and for prime 5, the sum must be \u2264 1.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to finding the number of ordered triples ( (a\u2081, b\u2081, c\u2081), (a\u2082, b\u2082, c\u2082), (a\u2083, b\u2083, c\u2083) ) where each (a_i, b_i, c_i) is a triple of exponents for the prime factors of ",
